window.FIELD_CHAPTER_PARTS["book-3-full-book"] = window.FIELD_CHAPTER_PARTS["book-3-full-book"] || {};
window.FIELD_CHAPTER_PARTS["book-3-full-book"][131] = "<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> analytics · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Intermediate · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C14 · <strong>Competency:</strong> AN · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> In an A/B test, time spent per user is up 6% and revenue per user is down 3%. What do you do?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Time spent is up, so ship,&quot; or &quot;revenue is down, so don't.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Asks which metric reflects the goal and whether each result is real.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Checks validity and intervals for both, uses the pre-set metric hierarchy, converts the revenue effect into dollars, investigates the mechanism and segments, adds a long-term holdout, and decides by a rule.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> <em>Validity first.</em> Check the split of users, the duration (full weekly cycles) and any novelty effect. Then read both results with intervals. Suppose 200,000 users per arm (invented). Time per user rose from 30.0 to 31.8 minutes (+1.8, a 6% gain); with a standard deviation of about 40 minutes the standard error of the difference is about 0.13, so the interval is roughly +1.55 to +2.05: clearly positive. Revenue per user fell from $2.00 to $1.94 (−$0.06); with a standard deviation of about $9 the standard error is about $0.028, giving an interval from about −$0.116 to −$0.004: a small but real fall, near the edge of detectability. <em>Which metric is the goal?</em> The test should have had a primary metric and guardrails written in advance (FW-C14-05). If the feature exists to raise engagement and revenue is a guardrail, a statistically real fall in the guardrail has to be explained, not waved away; if the product's business depends on revenue per user, revenue is the primary and time spent is a mechanism. <em>Convert to dollars.</em> With 5 million monthly users, −$0.06 per user is −$300,000 a month, or $3.6 million a year. The question is whether the extra 1.8 minutes buys something worth more: higher retention, more ad inventory or more purchases later. That is a hypothesis, not a result. <em>Find the mechanism.</em> Why would time rise and revenue fall? Perhaps the feature displaces purchase flows or ad slots (a mechanical effect), or it brings in light users who spend less (a mix effect), or it delays checkout. Break the result down by segment, by surface and by funnel step. If it is mix, the effect on retained revenue may be positive over time; if it is displacement, the feature may be trading revenue for minutes. <em>Check the long term.</em> Keep a long-term holdout and compare 90-day retention and revenue. Time spent is not value by itself; if retention does not move, the revenue loss is simply a cost. <em>Decision.</em> Follow the pre-set rule. If revenue was a guardrail with a margin tighter than the observed fall, do not ship as is; redesign to protect the purchase flow, or ship to the segments where revenue is not hurt, and run the long-term check. If there was no rule, set one now and say that it was a gap. What would change my view: evidence that retention and later revenue rise enough to repay $3.6 million a year. <em>Signals.</em> Both metrics read with intervals; the metric hierarchy; dollars; mechanism and mix; long-term holdout; a rule.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c15-01\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C15-01</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> execution · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Beginner · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C15 · <strong>Competency:</strong> EX · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> How do you prioritize features?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;I use RICE&quot; (or another framework), with nothing more.</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Starts from the goal, derives criteria, sizes options and decides.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Treats frameworks as tools, runs a sensitivity check, names the opportunity cost and the riskiest assumption, and says how stakeholders are brought along.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> I start with the goal, because the same list ranks differently under different goals. I ask what we are trying to move this quarter and what is fixed: capacity, the date, dependencies. Then I derive criteria from the goal: who must be affected, by how much on the goal metric, how sure we are, and what it costs. If the goal is new-user activation, reach means new users, not everyone. Then I size each option with a reason and a range, and I use a framework such as RICE only to organize the numbers: reach, impact, confidence, effort. I'd say where each number comes from and what the least certain one is. I rerun the ranking with that number changed; if the top option holds, I trust it, and if two options swap, I know that is where to spend discussion or a small test. I name the opportunity cost: choosing A means not building B, and I say what B was worth. I look for the riskiest assumption in the winner and the cheapest way to test it before a full build. I decide, say what we are not doing and when we'd revisit it, and I take the reasoning to stakeholders so a &quot;not now&quot; is understood, not imposed. The mistake I'd avoid is letting a score be the decision. A framework gives a way to talk about the options; the decision is a judgment about the goal, the evidence and the tradeoffs.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c15-02\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C15-02</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> execution · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Beginner · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C15 · <strong>Competency:</strong> EX · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> In a scoping meeting, every stakeholder says their items are &quot;must-haves.&quot; How do you handle it?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;I'd get everyone to agree&quot; or &quot;I'd decide myself.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Uses a tool such as MoSCoW with a constraint to force tradeoffs.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Anchors &quot;must&quot; to the goal and the date, uses capacity to test the list, makes tradeoffs visible, and leaves the decision with a named owner.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> When everything is a must, the word has lost its meaning, so I'd redefine it against a constraint. A &quot;must&quot; is something without which the release fails its goal or cannot ship at the date (a legal requirement, a core flow). I'd restate the goal and the date, then ask of each item: &quot;If this were missing at launch, would the release still deliver its goal?&quot; Then I'd bring capacity into the room. Suppose the &quot;musts&quot; total 40 person-weeks and we have 24 with a buffer. I'd say: &quot;These can't all be musts; we have capacity for 24. What leaves?&quot; Putting the number in front of people shifts the conversation from defending items to choosing among them. I'd use MoSCoW (must, should, could, won't this time) to sort, with the &quot;won't this time&quot; column given as much attention as &quot;must,&quot; because it shows people the item is deferred and not dismissed, with a date. For disputes I'd offer options with costs rather than argue: &quot;If we keep A, we lose C and D; if we keep C and D, A moves to the next release. Which serves the goal?&quot; The decision belongs to a named owner, usually me with the lead engineer and designer, with the sponsor informed. After the meeting, I'd send the written list with the reasons, so the criteria are on record. What would change my mind: new evidence that a deferred item is a blocker for the goal.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c15-03\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C15-03</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> execution · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Intermediate · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C15 · <strong>Competency:</strong> EX · <strong>Company tag:</strong> Apple</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> Three candidates for the next system release: better notifications, a new widget, a battery mode. Pick one.</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;The battery mode, because people care about battery.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> States a goal, derives criteria, compares the three on them, and picks one with a reason.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Shows the ranking depends on the goal, runs a sensitivity check, names tradeoffs (including effects on developers, privacy and other devices), tests the riskiest assumption, and says what would change the choice.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> I know the product only from public information; nothing here describes any real plan. I'd first ask what the release is meant to achieve, because the right pick depends on it. Assume the goal is to raise day-to-day satisfaction for the largest group of users across a range of devices, with a secondary aim of keeping developers engaged. I'd say this is my assumption and ask whether it is right. <em>Criteria from the goal.</em> How many users benefit, how much it improves their day (impact), how sure we are, how much it costs to build, and what it does to others: developers (a widget depends on them), the user's other devices, privacy, and the platform's consistency. <em>Sizing (invented).</em> Reach as a share of users, impact on a 0.25 to 3 scale, confidence, and effort in person-months. | Option | Reach | Impact | Confidence | Effort | Score | |---|---|---|---|---|---| | Battery mode | 60% | 2 | 70% | 4 | 21.0 | | Better notifications | 80% | 1 | 50% | 8 | 5.0 | | New widget | 40% | 1 | 60% | 5 | 4.8 | <em>Reasoning behind the numbers.</em> Battery mode reaches the people who run low in a day (most mobile users sometimes), has a measurable benefit (hours of use) and is a setting, which is lower risk to existing behavior. Notifications touch nearly everyone, but a better version is hard to define, so confidence is low, effort is high, and changes affect developers and the experience of users who like theirs (the legacy-user question). A widget depends on developers adopting it, so its reach is limited by their uptake. <em>Sensitivity.</em> The battery mode's impact is the key number. If it is 1 instead of 2, its score is 10.5, still more than twice the others. For the notifications to win, their confidence and effort would both have to be far better than assumed. So the choice is robust to this uncertainty. It would change if the goal changed: if the aim were to strengthen the developer ecosystem, the widget would rise, since it creates something for developers to build. <em>Riskiest assumption and test.</em> That users would turn on a battery mode and accept its tradeoffs (reduced performance or background activity). A cheap test is a prototype setting with a small group measuring opt-in and satisfaction, with a guardrail on complaints about slowness. I'd also check the interaction with other devices (does it sync across a user's devices?). <em>Decision.</em> &quot;I'd pick the battery mode: it serves the most users with the clearest benefit for the least cost and risk. I'd defer notifications until we can define the improvement, and the widget until developer demand is clearer. What would change my mind: evidence that the benefit is under half what I assumed, or a goal that favors the ecosystem.&quot; <em>Company note.</em> Apple is the setting only: <code>Inferred</code> from third-party reports that prioritization prompts at Apple involve choosing among candidate features for a release <code>[unverified: candidate-reported]</code>. No claim is made about any real release, plan or user research.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c15-04\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C15-04</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> execution · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Intermediate · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C15 · <strong>Competency:</strong> EX · <strong>Company tag:</strong> Microsoft</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> Three large enterprise customers each want a different thing: admin controls, better performance, more integrations. They are 12%, 9% and 6% of revenue. How do you prioritize?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Do the biggest customer's request first.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Separates revenue at risk from breadth of need and effort, and looks beyond the three.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Distinguishes blockers (compliance) from preferences, finds shared needs, sizes with a framework and sensitivity, protects against concentration and precedent, and plans communication.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> I'd avoid ranking by customer size, because it makes the roadmap a function of who shouts loudest and ignores the other 197 customers. My approach: separate what each request is <em>for</em>, how broad the need is, and what it costs. <em>Understand the needs.</em> For each customer, ask what outcome they want and what happens if they do not get it. The largest customer's admin controls might be a compliance requirement (a blocker, with a deadline) or a convenience. If it is a compliance blocker, it is a constraint: either we meet it or we risk the account; I'd then look for the smallest set of controls that satisfies the requirement. <em>Breadth.</em> Check the wider base. Suppose (invented) of 200 customers, 30 have asked for admin controls, 55 for performance and 80 for integrations. | Option | Customers (reach) | Impact | Confidence | Effort (pm) | Score | |---|---|---|---|---|---| | Integrations | 80 | 1 | 70% | 4 | 14.0 | | Performance | 55 | 2 | 60% | 9 | 7.3 | | Admin controls | 30 | 1 | 80% | 6 | 4.0 | Integrations score highest because of breadth and low effort. Performance has the highest impact per user but costs the most. Admin controls reach the fewest customers, but that count is not the whole story: the largest customer sits there. <em>Overlay revenue and risk.</em> The 12% customer is $X of revenue; if the need is a blocker, the expected loss from refusing (probability of leaving times revenue) may exceed the value of the next item, as in the break-even arithmetic in Worked example 5. I'd talk to them to learn the probability and the minimum viable set. <em>Decision.</em> Deliver the minimum compliance subset of admin controls first if it is a true blocker (perhaps 2.5 person-months), then integrations, then performance in stages, giving each customer a dated answer. I'd avoid a custom build for any one customer and prefer general features. I'd also address concentration: three customers are 27% of revenue, which is a business risk to reduce by serving the broader base. <em>Communicate.</em> Share the criteria and the order with all three, with what they get and when, and what the alternative is until then. What would change my mind: learning that the 9% customer's performance issue is causing active evaluation of a competitor. <em>Company note.</em> Microsoft is the setting only: <code>Inferred</code> from candidate reports that enterprise-prioritization prompts at Microsoft separate buyer and user needs <code>[unverified: candidate-reported]</code>. No Microsoft data or product behavior is claimed.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c15-05\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C15-05</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> execution · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Hard · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C15 · <strong>Competency:</strong> EX · <strong>Company tag:</strong> Meta</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> Two teams want the same engineers for the next quarter: the growth team, to run experiments to raise new-user engagement, and the integrity team, to reduce harmful content. How do you decide?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Whichever has the bigger numbers.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Sees that the goals are not in one unit, defines criteria that apply to both, and proposes an allocation.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Separates constraints (a minimum level of safety) from tradeable goals, uses guardrails and thresholds, sets a review date, and handles the politics with a decision rule and an escalation path.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> I know the product only from public information. The first thing I'd notice is that the two goals are not in the same unit: growth is measured in engagement, integrity in the prevalence of harmful content. Trading them with one score would be false precision. So I'd separate the question into what is a constraint and what is a choice. <em>Constraints first.</em> Some integrity work is not tradeable: legal requirements, commitments to regulators, and a minimum safety standard the company has set. I'd ask the integrity team which of their items are in that category. Those are funded first, as constraints, and the growth team cannot trade against them. <em>What remains is a choice.</em> For the rest, I'd define criteria that apply to both: contribution to the company's goals for the period, size of the expected effect with a range, confidence, cost of delay (what gets worse if we wait), and risk (including reputational and user harm). Cost of delay is important for integrity: a harm that grows each week is different from an experiment that can wait. <em>Sizing (invented).</em> Say there are 6 engineers. Growth proposes experiments with an expected +0.8% in new-user engagement, a 40% chance each of a positive result. Integrity proposes work to reduce harmful content views from 0.12% to 0.08% of views, a one-third reduction, with higher confidence. These cannot be compared in units, but each can be checked against a threshold the company has set (for example, a target prevalence). <em>Allocation.</em> Not all-or-nothing. A sensible split: fund integrity to the level that meets the constraint and the target, say 3 engineers, and give 3 to growth, with a guardrail on the growth work (no experiment ships that raises harm metrics beyond a set margin). If the integrity target is not met at 3, move more. Review at the midpoint of the quarter with data. <em>Decision rule and escalation.</em> If the two leads cannot agree, decide by a rule agreed in advance: constraints first, then expected contribution to company goals weighted by cost of delay. If they still disagree, escalate to the shared leader with a one-page tradeoff table, not a debate. <em>Why this is not a compromise by default.</em> A 50-50 split has no logic; the split follows from the constraint and the target. What would change my mind: new evidence that the harm is growing fast, which would move engineers to integrity, or a growth opportunity that expires. <em>Company note.</em> Meta is the setting only: <code>Inferred</code> from candidate reports that prioritization prompts at Meta can set growth and integrity goals against each other <code>[unverified: candidate-reported]</code>. No Meta data or internal practice is claimed.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c15-06\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C15-06</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> execution · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Hard · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C15 · <strong>Competency:</strong> EX · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> Option A is a sure, modest win. Option B is a big win that might not work. You have capacity for one. How do you choose?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;B has the bigger upside, so B,&quot; or &quot;A is safe, so A.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Compares expected values per unit of effort and considers risk appetite.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Computes expected value net of cost, shows how a cheap test changes the comparison, treats the probabilities as assumptions, and sequences the options.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> I'd compare expected value net of cost, then see whether a test changes the picture. Suppose (invented) A is a $2,000,000 annual benefit with a 60% chance of working, costing 4 person-months at $25,000 each ($100,000). B is a $5,000,000 benefit with a 20% chance, costing 10 person-months ($250,000). - A: 0.6 × $2,000,000 = $1,200,000, less $100,000, is $1,100,000. - B: 0.2 × $5,000,000 = $1,000,000, less $250,000, is $750,000. On expected value, A is ahead even though B's prize is bigger. Per person-month, A returns about $300,000 and B about $100,000. <em>Does a test change B?</em> The 20% chance is an assumption. Suppose a concierge or prototype test costs 1 person-month ($25,000) and reliably tells us whether B is viable. If we build B only when the test says yes (20% of the time), the expected value is 0.2 × $5,000,000 less 0.2 × $250,000 for the build, less $25,000 for the test: $1,000,000 − $50,000 − $25,000 = $925,000. The test improves B from $750,000 to $925,000 by avoiding the 80% of builds that would fail. B-with-test is still behind A's $1,100,000. <em>Decision.</em> Do A first. Run the cheap test of B in parallel if there is any spare capacity, because the test is worth more than it costs. The choice depends on the probabilities, so I'd state them as assumptions and invite challenge: for B to beat A, its chance of working would need to be above about 27% ($1,100,000 + $250,000 = $1,350,000, divided by $5,000,000 is 27%, ignoring the test). <em>Beyond the numbers.</em> Risk appetite matters. If the company needs a big bet (a strategic gap, a competitor), B's upside may matter more than expected value, and a portfolio view (several A's and a B) is better than choosing one. And the learning from testing B has option value. What would change my mind: evidence that A's 60% is overstated, or that B's market window is closing.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c15-07\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C15-07</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> execution · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Hard · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C15 · <strong>Competency:</strong> EX · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> A senior executive wants you to build their idea next. The data suggests another item is better. How do you handle it?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Do what the executive says,&quot; or &quot;refuse and cite the data.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Treats it as a decision with evidence, brings options and a recommendation, and uses a respectful script.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Seeks the need behind the idea, separates what the data shows from what it cannot, proposes a cheap test, offers a way to include the idea, and keeps the relationship and the decision rights clear.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> I'd begin by assuming the executive knows something I don't: a customer conversation, a strategic bet, a competitor's move. So the first step is to ask about the need behind the idea, not to defend my list. &quot;Help me understand what you're seeing that makes this the priority.&quot; Often the idea is a solution to a problem that can be solved in more than one way. <em>Show the tradeoff, not just my view.</em> I'd bring the options with their costs. Say the executive's idea is X and my data points to Y. A one-page table: expected impact on the agreed goal with a range, effort, confidence, and what each displaces. &quot;If we build X, we defer Y, which we estimate at $A a year; X's value depends on assumption Z, which we have not tested.&quot; <em>Be honest about the data's limits.</em> Data on past behavior does not capture a new strategic bet. If the executive's case is strategic, my numbers on near-term impact are not the whole argument. I'd say what the data does and does not show. <em>Offer routes that respect the executive.</em> (1) A cheap test of X's riskiest assumption in two weeks, with success and kill criteria agreed beforehand; (2) a smaller version of X that fits alongside Y; (3) a sequence, Y now and X next quarter with a date. These let the executive see that their idea was taken seriously. <em>Use the script (FW-C15-07).</em> &quot;I see the need: [need]. To build X now we'd delay Y, which means [consequence]. What I can do is [test or smaller version]. If the test shows [result], I'd move X up.&quot; And keep the decision rights clear: if they decide to proceed after seeing the tradeoff, I'd do it well and record the assumption so we learn from the outcome, without saying &quot;I told you so.&quot; <em>What I'd avoid.</em> Hiding the tradeoff to avoid conflict, quietly deprioritizing, or turning it into a contest of status. What would change my mind: the executive revealing a fact (a commitment, a customer deadline) that changes the value of X.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c15-08\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C15-08</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> execution · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Intermediate · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C15 · <strong>Competency:</strong> EX · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> A feature used by 4% of weekly users takes about one and a half engineers to maintain. Should you retire it?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Yes, it's a small share,&quot; or &quot;no, someone uses it.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Looks at who uses it, the cost, and the alternatives.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Quantifies the cost against the revenue at risk, finds the break-even churn, considers lighter options (maintenance mode, simplification, migration), plans communication and a reversible rollout, and sets a decision rule.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> <em>Who uses it, and why.</em> 4% of 2 million weekly users is 80,000 people. Share of users says little by itself: what matters is who they are. Are they heavy, loyal or high-value users (suppose their retention is 92% against 70% overall)? Do they use it for a job nothing else does? Are they concentrated in a segment we care about? <em>Cost against what is at risk (invented figures).</em> At about $15,000 a month per engineer, one and a half engineers cost $22,500 a month, plus the opportunity cost of what they would otherwise build. If these users pay about $4 a month and some leave when the feature goes, the revenue at risk is the number who churn times $4. The break-even is 22,500 ÷ (80,000 × 4) = about 7.0% of those users leaving. If 5% leave, retiring saves $22,500 and loses $16,000, a net gain of $6,500 a month; if 10% leave, it loses $32,000, a net loss of $9,500; at 20%, a net loss of $41,500. So the decision turns on the churn we would cause, which I can estimate rather than guess. <em>Options between keep and kill.</em> (A) Keep as is. (B) Put it in maintenance mode: freeze features, fix only critical bugs, and cut the cost to perhaps half an engineer. (C) Simplify or fold its core into another feature. (D) Retire with a migration path, a long notice period and an export. (E) Retire outright. <em>How I'd estimate churn.</em> Look at what these users do instead when the feature is unavailable (a holdout or a past outage), survey the heaviest users, and test a deprecation notice with a small group to see who leaves. Also check dependencies: other features, integrations and support load. <em>Recommendation.</em> Start with B and measure; if the estimated churn from retirement is under about 5% and the freed capacity has a clear, better use, move to D with notice and migration. If the users are high-value and the churn estimate is above the break-even, keep it in maintenance mode and revisit when the cost or use changes. I'd decide with a rule agreed in advance and tell users plainly what is happening and why. <em>Signals.</em> Who uses it; cost against revenue at risk; a computed break-even; options between keep and kill; communication and reversibility.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c16-01\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C16-01</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> execution · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Beginner · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C16 · <strong>Competency:</strong> EX · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> What goes into a good PRD?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;The requirements and the designs.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Lists the sections (problem, goals, non-goals, requirements, metrics, risks) and says why each exists.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Distinguishes good from bad examples, scales length to stakes, treats the document as a living tool for alignment, and mentions the decision log and the partners.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> A PRD exists to align a team on what to build and why, so the best test of one is whether someone new could read it and explain the problem, the goal and what is out of scope. <em>Problem.</em> Who has what problem, with evidence, and the cost of leaving it. No solution in it: &quot;Shoppers who search by name get no results on 18% of queries, and 60% leave&quot; and not &quot;we need a better search.&quot; <em>Goals and metrics.</em> The outcome and how we will measure it: a primary metric with a threshold, a guardrail, and a counter metric (C05). <em>Non-goals.</em> Things a reasonable stakeholder might have expected that we are not doing in this version. They prevent scope creep and end arguments early. <em>Users and stories.</em> Who is affected, and the key scenarios as short stories with acceptance criteria that QA can test. <em>Requirements.</em> In priority order (must, should, could), each specific enough that an engineer can estimate it and a tester can test it: &quot;results load in under 1.5 seconds at the 95th percentile,&quot; not &quot;fast.&quot; <em>Risks, assumptions and open questions.</em> The riskiest assumption and the cheap test for it, what could go wrong, and what is undecided with an owner and a date. <em>Launch and measurement.</em> How it ships (staged), who decides go or no-go and on what criteria, how results will be read. I'd scale it to the stakes: two pages for a small feature, longer with a summary on top for a multi-team launch. I'd keep a decision log at the top and update the document as we learn, and I'd review it with the engineering lead and designer before anyone commits. The mistake is writing a long document that no one reads or a feature list with no problem. Those are signals that the spec is for the author, not the team.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c16-02\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C16-02</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> execution · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Beginner · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C16 · <strong>Competency:</strong> EX · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> You expected a feature to take four weeks. Engineering says twelve. What do you do?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Push back and ask them to find ways to be faster,&quot; or &quot;accept it.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Asks why, gets a range and assumptions, and looks at scope options.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Uses the outside view, separates complexity from risk, proposes a smaller first slice and checkpoints, and avoids pressure on the estimate.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> A threefold gap between what I expected and what engineering says is information. Either my expectation was missing something about the system, or the scope is bigger than I realized. I'd start with curiosity, not pressure. <em>Understand.</em> I'd ask for the breakdown: where does the time go, what is the riskiest part, what assumptions does 12 weeks rest on, and what is the range? Suppose it is 8 to 16 weeks, driven by a data model that does not exist yet. Then I'd learn that my four-week picture assumed reuse of something that is not there. <em>Check my side.</em> What did I assume, and from what? If I had a reason (a similar feature took four weeks), compare with this one: what differs? <em>Use the outside view.</em> How have this team's estimates compared with actuals? If their past projects ran 1.1 to 2.0 times their estimates, 12 weeks may itself be optimistic, which means planning for four is not realistic for the full scope. <em>Negotiate scope, not the number.</em> If I need something in four to six weeks, ask: &quot;What is the smallest version that delivers the core of the user's job, and how long does it take?&quot; Perhaps a first slice that handles the common case in 4 to 5 weeks, with the full version later. Or a two-day technical spike to narrow the range before we commit. <em>Decide and communicate.</em> Option 1: ship the smaller slice in five weeks and the remainder in a follow-up. Option 2: keep full scope and plan 12 to 16 weeks, with a checkpoint at week 3. I'd explain the tradeoff to stakeholders with the range and the reason. What I would not do is ask engineers to promise four weeks for the full scope. That produces hidden shortcuts and a late surprise.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c16-03\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C16-03</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> execution · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Intermediate · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C16 · <strong>Competency:</strong> EX · <strong>Company tag:</strong> Microsoft</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> You are shipping admin audit logs in an enterprise product. Legal wants 180-day retention, security wants the logs encrypted and access-controlled, finance is worried about storage cost, and support wants training. How do you coordinate?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Hold a meeting with everyone and get alignment.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Identifies what each partner needs and when, and sequences the work.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Puts numbers on the cost tradeoff, spots a conflict between requirements (retention versus erasure requests), proposes options and a decision owner, and plans a staged rollout with design partners.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> I know the product only from public information; nothing here describes any real plan. I'd treat each partner as holding a constraint that is cheap to meet early and expensive late, and sequence accordingly. <em>Legal and privacy first.</em> Retention length and the handling of personal data shape the design. They may also create a conflict: logs kept 180 days for audit may contain personal data that a person can ask to have erased in some jurisdictions <code>[V: GDPR Article 17 right to erasure, unless retention is legally required, as summarized in guidance]</code>. I'd ask legal for the rule, not guess, and put the answer in the spec (for example, logs record identifiers that can be pseudonymized on request, with the audit trail intact). <em>Security.</em> Design review of how logs are written (tamper-resistant), who can read them (administrators only, with their own access logged), and encryption. Early involvement avoids a high-risk finding in the week before launch. <em>Finance.</em> Put numbers on retention. Suppose (invented) storage costs $4 per customer per month at 90 days and $8 at 180 days; for 2,000 customers that is $8,000 against $16,000 a month, $96,000 a year more. Options to put to finance and product leadership: include 90 days in all plans and sell 180 days as a higher tier; include 180 for all and absorb the cost; or tier by customer size. I'd bring the options with the cost and the demand evidence (how many customers need 180 days), not a request. <em>Support and operations.</em> Training, scripts for the questions they will receive (&quot;why can't I see events older than 90 days?&quot;), and an escalation route. Involve them two to three weeks before launch. <em>Engineering and design.</em> Confirm the event stream carries the data; design search and filters. <em>Sequence and ownership.</em> Week 1: legal, security and finance decisions with a named owner each. Weeks 2 to 6: build. Week 5: support training. Launch: staged, first to ten design-partner customers, with go or no-go criteria in the spec (no S1 defects, search within the performance target, support trained). I'd keep a decision log and a short weekly note to all partners. <em>Where I'd decide.</em> If legal and finance conflict, I'd bring the options to the shared leader with a recommendation. What would change my plan: legal saying that a particular market needs a different retention rule, which would turn retention into a regional setting. <em>Company note.</em> Microsoft is the setting only: <code>Inferred</code> from candidate reports that enterprise-product prompts involve compliance and security partners <code>[unverified: candidate-reported]</code>. No Microsoft data or product behavior is claimed.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c16-04\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C16-04</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> execution · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Intermediate · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C16 · <strong>Competency:</strong> EX · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> Your engineering lead and your designer both think your plan is wrong, for different reasons. What do you do?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Overrule them&quot; or &quot;do what they say.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Listens to each, restates the shared goal, and looks for evidence.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Separates the criteria each is using, finds the cheapest test, names the decision owner, commits afterward, and keeps the relationships intact.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> When two of my closest partners disagree with my plan, I'd take it as a strong signal and not as an obstacle. I'd talk to each separately first, then together. <em>Listen for the criteria.</em> The engineer thinks it is too risky or too big: the criterion is cost and reliability. The designer thinks the experience will confuse users: the criterion is user value and usability. They may be seeing different parts of the same flaw. I'd ask each: &quot;What would you do instead, and what are you worried will happen if we go with my plan?&quot; <em>Restate the shared goal.</em> &quot;We all want [goal] by [date]. Do we agree on that?&quot; If there is disagreement about the goal itself, that is the real conflict and I'd resolve it first. <em>Find the cheapest evidence.</em> Is there a way to test the contested points quickly? A paper prototype tested with five users answers the designer's concern in two days; a technical spike answers the engineer's in two or three. I'd propose both and agree beforehand what result would change the plan. <em>Decide and own it.</em> If the evidence is clear, change the plan and say what I learned. If it is not, I decide, since the call on scope and priority is mine, and I explain the reasoning and the risk I am accepting, and invite them to say what would make us revisit. Then we commit: &quot;disagree and commit,&quot; with a date to review. <em>Protect the relationship.</em> I'd thank them for the challenge, credit their input in the final plan, and make sure that raising concerns early is rewarded. What I'd avoid is deciding by rank, hiding the disagreement or reopening the decision every week. What would change my mind: any evidence that the plan fails the user or cannot be built in time.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c16-05\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C16-05</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> execution · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Hard · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C16 · <strong>Competency:</strong> EX · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> Your engineers ask for a full quarter to refactor a core system. Sales wants three features. How do you decide?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Features, because they drive revenue,&quot; or &quot;refactor, because debt is dangerous.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Asks for the cost of the debt and compares it with the features.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Computes payback and the effect of risk, shows sensitivity, proposes staging with a measure, and communicates the tradeoff to sales.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> I'd refuse to treat it as engineering against sales and ask the same question of both: what does each return for the capacity it uses? For the refactoring I need the principal, the interest, and the risk. <em>Numbers (invented).</em> Six engineers, two-week sprints: 12 person-weeks a sprint. The refactor costs 24 person-weeks (two sprints, the principal). Workarounds cost about 10% of capacity, 1.2 person-weeks per sprint (the interest). Payback on capacity alone: 24 ÷ 1.2 = 20 sprints, about 40 weeks. Over a year (26 sprints), capacity savings are 26 × 1.2 = 31.2 person-weeks, more than the 24 spent: a net of +7.2 person-weeks on time savings alone, and payback takes the first 20 sprints of the year. <em>But debt also carries risk.</em> Suppose the system has two serious incidents a year, each costing $150,000 in lost revenue and recovery, and the refactor would roughly halve them: $150,000 a year of avoided loss. At about $15,000 per person-week of engineering value (invented), the time savings are 31.2 × $15,000 = $468,000, plus $150,000 of avoided incidents, $618,000 a year, against a cost of 24 × $15,000 = $360,000. So the year-one return is about +$258,000, and over two years it is $1,236,000 against $360,000, a gain of $876,000. The case is positive in year one and strong over two, if the interest and the incident rate hold. <em>Compare with the features.</em> Size the three sales features the same way (C15.5): expected value per person-week. If they return more than $15,000 per person-week, they win this year; if less, the refactor is at least as good. <em>Sensitivity.</em> The interest and the incident rate are the uncertain numbers. If interest is 5% rather than 10%, the time savings halve to $234,000 and the year-one return is about break-even (+$24,000), and if the incident saving also disappears it is −$126,000. So I'd ask the team to measure the interest: hours lost to the system over the past quarter, and the incident history. <em>Decision.</em> Not a whole quarter of nothing else. Stage it: do the highest-interest part first, perhaps 8 person-weeks, with a measure of hours saved and incidents after, and ship the most valuable one or two sales features in the same quarter. Tell sales what they get and when, and why the refactor is part of reliability they rely on. Review at the end of the first stage. What would change my mind: a major incident in the system, which would move the whole refactor up, or a sales feature tied to a signed commitment.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c16-06\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C16-06</h3>\n";
